---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import BreadcrumbNav from '../../components/BreadcrumbNav.astro';
import AuthorByline from '../../components/AuthorByline.astro';
import FAQSection from '../../components/FAQSection.astro';
import MarkCompleteButton from '../../components/MarkCompleteButton.astro';
import LessonFooterNav from '../../components/LessonFooterNav.astro';
import { authorship, learningResource } from '../../lib/schema';
import { lessonByN } from '../../lib/lessons';

const lesson = lessonByN(1);
const DATE_PUBLISHED = '2026-07-29';
const DATE_MODIFIED = '2026-07-29';

const faqs = [
  { question: 'What is Medicare?', answer: 'Medicare is the federal health insurance program for people 65 and older, and certain younger people with disabilities, End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD), or ALS. It\'s administered by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS).' },
  { question: 'What are the 4 parts of Medicare?', answer: 'Part A (hospital insurance), Part B (medical insurance), Part C (Medicare Advantage, a private-plan alternative), and Part D (prescription drug coverage). Parts A and B together are called Original Medicare.' },
  { question: 'Do I need all 4 parts?', answer: 'Most people need Part A and Part B at minimum. From there, you choose either a Medicare Advantage plan (which often bundles Part D) or a standalone Part D plan alongside Original Medicare. Skipping drug coverage entirely can lead to a lasting penalty.' },
  { question: 'Is Medicare the same as Medicaid?', answer: 'No. Medicare is federal and based on age or disability, not income. Medicaid is a joint federal-state program based on financial need. See our full comparison for the complete breakdown.' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  learningResource({
    name: lesson.title,
    description: lesson.description,
    url: `https://learnmedicare.org${lesson.path}/`,
    position: lesson.n,
  }),
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: lesson.title,
    ...authorship(DATE_PUBLISHED, DATE_MODIFIED),
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Lesson 1: What Is Medicare? | Free Medicare Course"
  description="Lesson 1 of our free Medicare course: what Medicare is, who qualifies, and how Parts A, B, C, and D fit together — explained in plain English."
  path="/course/lesson-1/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Take the Course', url: '/course/' }, { name: 'Lesson 1: What Is Medicare?', url: '/course/lesson-1/' }]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <BreadcrumbNav crumbs={[{ name: 'Take the Course', url: '/course/' }, { name: 'Lesson 1', url: '/course/lesson-1/' }]} />
  <PageHero
    label="Lesson 1 of 6"
    heading="What Is Medicare?"
    subtext="The foundation for everything else in this course — what Medicare is, who qualifies, and how its four parts fit together."
  >
    <AuthorByline date={DATE_MODIFIED} datePublished={DATE_PUBLISHED} />
  </PageHero>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="quick-answer">
        <p class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</p>
        <p>
          Medicare is the U.S. federal health insurance program for people 65 and older, and
          certain younger people with disabilities. It's made up of four parts — A, B, C, and D —
          that together cover hospital stays, doctor visits, and prescription drugs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Who Qualifies?</h2>
      <p>
        You generally qualify for Medicare at age 65 if you or a spouse worked and paid Medicare
        taxes for at least 10 years. You can also qualify earlier with 24 months of Social Security
        Disability Insurance (SSDI), or immediately with End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) or ALS.
        Income has no bearing on basic eligibility — this is one of the biggest differences between
        Medicare and Medicaid.
      </p>

      <h2>The 4 Parts, at a Glance</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Part A — Hospital Insurance</h3>
          <p>
            Covers inpatient hospital stays, skilled nursing facility care, hospice, and some home
            health care. Usually premium-free if you have enough work history. We'll go deeper on
            Part A in Lesson 2.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Part B — Medical Insurance</h3>
          <p>
            Covers doctor visits, outpatient care, preventive services, and durable medical
            equipment. Most people pay a standard monthly premium ($202.90 in 2026).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Part C — Medicare Advantage</h3>
          <p>
            A private-plan alternative to Original Medicare, bundling A, B, and often D through an
            approved insurance company. We'll compare it to Original Medicare fully in Lesson 3.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Part D — Prescription Drug Coverage</h3>
          <p>
            Helps cover medication costs, either standalone or bundled into a Medicare Advantage
            plan. Lesson 4 covers exactly how this works, including the 2026 out-of-pocket cap.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <p style="margin:0;">
          <strong>Key term:</strong> "Original Medicare" means Parts A and B together, run directly
          by the federal government. Everything else in this course builds on that foundation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What Medicare Doesn't Cover</h2>
      <p>
        A common misconception is that Medicare covers everything once you turn 65. Original
        Medicare generally does not cover routine dental, vision, or hearing care, long-term
        custodial care, or most care outside the U.S. — and it has no annual out-of-pocket maximum.
        These gaps are exactly why Medicare Advantage plans and Medigap policies exist, which we'll
        cover in Lessons 3 and 5.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How This Course Is Organized</h2>
      <p>
        This lesson gave you the big picture. From here, each lesson goes one level deeper:
        Lesson 2 covers Part A and Part B in detail, Lesson 3 compares Medicare Advantage to
        Original Medicare, Lesson 4 explains Part D drug coverage, Lesson 5 covers Medigap
        supplement plans, and Lesson 6 walks through exactly when and how to enroll. For a broader,
        single-page version of this material, see our <a href="/what-is-medicare">What Is
        Medicare? guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Why Getting This Right Matters</h2>
      <p>
        Medicare's structure can feel like alphabet soup at first, but understanding it isn't just
        academic — it directly affects your wallet and your care. Enrolling late, or in the wrong
        combination of parts for your situation, can mean permanent late enrollment penalties added
        to your premium for as long as you have that coverage. Choosing between Medicare Advantage
        and Original Medicare without understanding the tradeoffs can mean discovering your
        preferred doctor isn't in-network, or facing unexpectedly high costs in a bad health year.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal of this course isn't to make you an expert on Medicare policy — it's to make sure
        you understand enough to ask the right questions and avoid the most common, most costly
        mistakes. If you want the short version of what those mistakes look like before working
        through the full course, see our <a href="/medicare-mistakes">Top 7 Medicare Mistakes to
        Avoid</a> guide.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqs} />

  <MarkCompleteButton slug={lesson.slug} />

  <section class="section-alt cta-band">
    <div class="container cta-inner">
      <h2>Have questions about your specific situation?</h2>
      <p>
        Kayla Price is a licensed insurance agent (NPN 18530055) offering free, no-pressure
        consultations. Call <a href="tel:8666481578">(866) 648-1578</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <LessonFooterNav n={lesson.n} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .cta-band { text-align: center; }
  .cta-inner p { color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 0; }

  .quick-answer {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--fill-accent);
    background: var(--fill-purple-light);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .quick-answer {
    background: rgba(144, 89, 181, 0.1);
  }
  .quick-answer-label {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--fill-accent);
  }
  .quick-answer p:last-child { margin: 0; }
</style>
